  <h1>Beyond research</h1>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Most of the stuff on this page is about detours and exploration. The company that came out of one of those
      detours has its own page — <a href="/clusy/">Clusy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Teaching and mentoring</h2>
    <p>
      I spent most of my Berkeley degree on the other side of the classroom.
      I was a teaching assistant for ENGIN 125 (Ethics, Engineering and
      Society), and a mentor with
      <a href="https://csmentors.studentorg.berkeley.edu/#/">Computer Science
      Mentors</a> for CS 61B (Data Structures and Algorithms) across three
      terms, first as a junior and then a senior mentor. I also worked as an
      EECS department private tutor for CS 61B and CS 161 (Computer Security),
      and tutored CS 161 for the SEED Scholars Honors Program. Teaching the
      same data-structures material to very different rooms is a large part of
      why the education-tools research on the
      <a href="/research/">research page</a> exists.
      Education and teaching remains a big passion of mine. 
    </p>

    <h2>Open source</h2>
    <p>
      I also maintain and support a bunch of open-source projects. I contribute to
      <a href="https://prairielearn.readthedocs.io/en/latest/">PrairieLearn</a>
      through
      <a href="https://github.com/eldarhasanov079/pl-interactive-graph-v2">interactive graph assessments</a>
      and a
      <a href="https://github.com/eldarhasanov079/pl-graph-constructor">graph constructor</a>
      that let students work graph problems without paper sketches; a
      <a href="https://github.com/eldarhasanov079/dimacs-sat-fuzzer">coverage-guided DIMACS SAT fuzzer</a>
      grew out of poking at solver reliability; a
      <a href="https://github.com/eldarhasanov079/secure-file-sharing-go">Go file-sharing client</a>
      explores end-to-end encryption with invites and revocation; and a
      <a href="https://github.com/eldarhasanov079/riscv-assembly-inference-engine">RISC-V assembly inference engine</a>
      is the same matrix pipeline, written without a higher-level language to
      hide behind.
    </p>

    <h2>Hackathons</h2>
    <p>
      I have competed in more than fifteen hackathons (CalHacks, TreeHacks,
      and SFHacks among them) and kept going after moving to London. Some of
      those ended up in awards, such as 1st places at IDDA Hackathon 2 and CulTech, Best Technical Implementation at Imperial's
      Learning Analytics Hackathon, top 5/155 on the UK Agentic AI Hackathon, and twice 3rd place on Netcraft's and Verno Labs security challenges.
    </p>

