    <h2>The route here</h2>
    <div class="route">
      <figure class="lead__figure route__figure">
        <img
          src="/img/childhood2.jpeg"
          alt="Toddler in blue pajamas sitting at a desk, hands on a keyboard in front of a Samsung SyncMaster CRT monitor"
          width="896"
          height="1280"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <figcaption>Baku, early on.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        I am from Baku, Azerbaijan. I was fascinated with computers from an
        early age, even though most of the tech around me was from the ’80s
        and ’90s. From PlayStation 1 and 2 and Windows 98 machines, I
        was always curious how these magical systems worked underneath. At
        around 14, after properly learning English, I discovered
        <a href="https://www.khanacademy.org/">Khan Academy</a>, where I
        first learned to code — thanks to tutorials from
        <a href="https://pamelafox.org/">Pamela Fox</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        After completing my A-levels in Oxford I moved to the Bay Area for
        Berkeley. It was a big move, with a lot of uncertainty for me. One of my
        very first classes was
        <a href="https://cs61a.org/">CS 61A</a>. On the first day in the
        lecture hall, the instructor had a very familiar voice, and after a
        while it kicked in that this was the same Pamela Fox whose videos I
        had watched online from my Windows XP laptop. Right then and there
        I knew it would be fine. Thanks to Berkeley, I got to meet (and even take classes from) people I
        had admired from afar, including
        <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Patterson_(computer_scientist)">David Patterson</a>,
        <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ion_Stoica">Ion Stoica</a>,
        <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matei_Zaharia">Matei Zaharia</a>,
        <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_Vixie">Paul Vixie</a>,
        <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stuart_J._Russell">Stuart Russell</a>,
        <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_M._Karp">Richard Karp</a>, and others.
      </p>
      <p>
        I then moved to London for master's — and these days I am back and
        forth between San Francisco and London. That journey across places and cultures
        has shaped not only how I think as an engineer, but how I see
        education, global issues, and people.
      </p>
    </div>

